  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Light rain across the northern tier overnight  \ninto Thursday, trending closer to climatological averages to \nbegin October.\n\nRain already falling in NWrn PA, and likely almost down to the\nsfc in our NW counties. Moisture plume stays highest along the\nnrn tier of counties tonight, and after an initial moistening-\nup period, rain should fall in many places N of I-80 tonight. \nTiming still looks the same, and amounts still look light - with\n0.10\" the most anyone should receive - with most receiving just\na wetting/trace. Did up the sky cover since it is already very \ncloudy over the NW half of the CWA. Some thinning is the high \novercast is likely through the night, so just kept it mostly \ncloudy there. \n\nPrevious...\nAn upper-level trough will bring some light rain across the \nnorthern tier overnight into Thursday morning. At this time, \nbest odds based on a combination of NBM/HREF guidance hones in \non areas north of Interstate 80 as the most likely to experience\nrainfall overnight. Some members of hi-res model guidance do \nindicate that rainfall could reach as far south as a JST/RVL/ZER\nline; however, forecast continues to outline areas north of \nI-80 as the most plausible to receive measurable rainfall. In \nterms of rainfall amounts, generally light rainfall is expected \nwith maximum rainfall amounts overnight topping out near 0.10\".\n\nMore widespread rainfall is projected by all model guidance\nalong a cold frontal passage. Timing remains fairly consistent\nbetween model guidance, with rainfall beginning closer to\nsunrise on Friday. Based on more recent model guidance, areas \nwest of US-219 could experience first raindrops just ahead of\nsunrise, thus have sped up mentions of rainfall in the most\ncurrent forecast package. Guidance remains fairly consistent on\nthe cold front diving southeast throughout the day on Friday\nwith rain chances beginning to exit the forecast area Friday\nevening. Instability associated with this frontal passage appear\nto be minimal; therefore, no thunderstorm mentions in the\nforecast package. \n\nOutside of a wave tracking south of the region on Sunday,\ntranquil weather conditions are expected for the region this\nweekend. With regards to the wave, best chances for\nprecipitation will be south of the Pennsylvania-Maryland border\nbut cannot completely rule out a shower south of the Turnpike on\nSunday, especially late Sunday morning into the early evening\nhours. Any additional rainfall will be light, before dry \nconditions prevail Sunday night and continue through the end of \nthe forecast period.\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2: Frost/freeze concerns across portions of central\nPennsylvania early next week, beginning on Monday night and \ncontinuing through Tuesday night.\n\nEarly next week, Canadian high pressure begins to build into \nthe region, bringing much cooler air and dry weather to the \nregion. LREF ensemble guidance outlines 850mb temperatures \ntrending close to 0F across portions of northern Pennsylvania as\nearly as Monday night, outlining the cooler temperatures that \nare expected for the beginning of the week. This high pressure \nwill lead to very light wind and mainly clear skies across much \nof central Pennsylvania, so the biggest question mark with \nrespect to frost will be how low temperatures get and if this is\nsufficient for frost formation. At this time, current forecast \noutlines MinTs closer to freezing north/west of the Allegheny \nFront confidence is higher in some frost/freeze concerns. \nFurther south/east, there is a larger spread in guidance with \nrespect to temperatures, so confidence remains lower; however, \ngenerally would urge those with agricultural interests to \ncontinue to monitor the forecast closely this weekend into the \nbeginning of next week.",
